{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfpn3GqbvR2GQxmom29WG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — \"never\" is too strong, and I'd be overclaiming if I let you hand me that version. The stronger the claim, the less falsifiable it gets, and \"a green suite still isn't really done\" is a sentence you can say forever about anything. That's not rigor, it's a way of making \"done\" carry no information at all. If nothing can discharge the done-signal, nobody will trust it when it fires.\n\nThere's a real class where a passing suite is genuinely sufficient: **when the suite is the contract rather than a sample of it.** A regression test that reproduces a reported bug exactly — that test *is* the acceptance criterion, green means done, full stop. A behavior-preserving refactor against a suite that was already trusted — the invariant is \"nothing changed,\" and the suite is the right instrument for exactly that. Acceptance criteria negotiated up front as tests, by someone with standing to define them: the judgment call happened at authoring time, and green discharges it.\n\nSo the discriminator isn't the green bar, it's **where the tests came from**. Tests written after the fact, from the implementation, by the person who wrote it, mostly encode what the code already does — they'll happily ratify a bug. Tests written beforehand from the spec, by someone reasoning about intent, encode a claim about what *should* happen. Identical green bar, completely different evidential weight. Provenance is the thing I'd actually ask about.\n\nWhich cuts against me here, so let me say it plainly: the slugify tests I just wrote are the stronger kind — written first, from the issue text, before any implementation — and they are still *demonstrably* insufficient. All seven go green on an implementation that turns `café` into `caf`. That's not a contrived failure; it's the most natural reading of the issue as literally written. Green bar, spec satisfied, data quietly mangled. The suite cannot report the hole, because a suite only ever tells you that none of the things someone thought to check are broken. The size of the unchecked space is invisible from inside.\n\nI think the reason this feels paradoxical is that two things are getting fused. **Done is a property of the handoff; correct is a property of the artifact.** A suite can fully settle done — we agreed on the contract, it's met, you can build on it — while the artifact is still wrong, because the contract was wrong. Those come apart, and holding \"done\" hostage to correctness means nothing ever ships.\n\nSo my actual position: a passing suite is sufficient for done exactly to the extent the suite states intent rather than samples it — and the one thing it can never do is tell you which of those it is."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3918,"cache_read_input_tokens":23599,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3918},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dee3fa8-e013-4c60-abd2-86ba54045ca3","uuid":"bce160da-9656-4e0f-b528-5d547f4a848a","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:34:38.273Z","request_id":"req_011Cfpn3Gay6UkxKcWEPRenz"}
